ade, a range (\u22120.01 to +0.11) that includes zero. It's close, but the range still includes zero. <strong>The last 40 years tell a different story:</strong> since 1986, lows have risen <strong class='sig'>+0.34\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.09 to +0.59) and highs show no clear change. The full record joins different observing sites over the years, and a move (for example, from the city to a cooler airport) can hide or exaggerate a long-run trend, so the recent decades are the clearer guide here.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 1 of 12 months; 4 months fell. Highs rose in 3 of 12 months; July fell.","t_rain":"Astoria has gotten drier","p_rain":"Astoria's yearly precipitation has fallen <strong class='sig'>\u22120.97 inches per decade</strong> (95% range \u22121.52 to \u22120.41), about \u221213.2 inches since 1889. The 1991\u20132020 normal is 70.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1933 (114.03 in); the driest, 1985 (41.58 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":false,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 2 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}